// Box2D for sysl — 2D rigid body physics, bound to https://github.com/erincatto/box2d.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because Box2D depends on nothing: it is C17 with no external
// libraries, and its source is carried in this package. Box2D is not in Homebrew and there is no
// system copy on a microcontroller, so vendoring is forced rather than chosen — and it is what
// `qcbor`, `plutovg`, `qoi` and `termbox2` already do.
//
// **The version is pinned to the v3.1.1 tag rather than to `main`.** `main` carries a
// `BOX2D_DOUBLE_PRECISION` mode that changes `b2Vec2`'s layout, which is `termbox2`'s
// `TB_OPT_ATTR_W` case exactly: an option that reaches a declaration. If it is ever wanted it goes
// in a vendored `c/options.h` that both the `.c` build and the `@include` name, rather than being
// left to whatever the consumer's preprocessor happened to have.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       box2d { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/box2d", version = "0.1.3" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.2` on this repository.

package {
  name    = "box2d"
  version = "0.1.3"

  // **The oldest compiler this builds with, and it is a released one.** `types.sysl` names Box2D's
  // shapes, ids, defs and events from the pleasant layer with `type Polygon = c.Polygon` — a
  // transparent alias over a struct, which arrived in 0.0.67. An older compiler refuses the file
  // outright, so the floor is here rather than left to a reader to work out from a parse error.
  //
  // **0.0.69 raised it.** Five of those names are also variants of `ShapeKind` or `JointKind`, and
  // until that release a call spelling one reached the variant whatever the site expected — so
  // `defs.sysl`'s builders had to construct through `c` and a caller could not write
  // `Circle(centre, r)` at all. They build by name now, which 0.0.68 refuses.
  //
  // **0.0.96 raised it again, for the scalar on the left.** `2.0 * v` needs an `impl` whose subject
  // is `f32` and whose trait argument is `Vec2`; coherence used to look only at the subject, so that
  // block had a home in no module at all and `math.sysl` said so in a comment. It counts the trait's
  // arguments now, and a block naming `Vec2` belongs where `Vec2` is declared.
  sysl = "0.0.96"
}

// **There is no shim.c, and that is the headline fact about this binding.**
//
// Every one of Box2D's 466 `B2_API` entry points takes scalars, pointers, or structs by value —
// and since 0.0.55 sysl can spell all three, in both directions, including a function pointer whose
// parameters are aggregates. So the `.c` files in `sh/sysl/box2d/c/` are Box2D's own, unedited, and
// this package adds no C at all.
//
// The seventy-nine `static inline` math functions are the one thing that would ordinarily need
// wrapping, since an inline function has no symbol to link (`15 §7`). They are written in sysl
// instead, in `sh.sysl.box2d.math`, where they become operators rather than calls.

requires {
  heap  = true
  posix = true
}

// **`heap` is honest and not currently avoidable.** Box2D allocates its worlds, its body and shape
// pools, its broad-phase trees and its solver's arenas. `b2SetAllocator` does exist and would let a
// caller point all of it at storage of its own, the way `qoi`'s `QOI_MALLOC` was redirected to get
// `requires {}` out of an allocating API — but Box2D's allocations are long-lived and of many sizes
// rather than one arena-shaped request, so it is a real allocator that would have to be supplied
// and not a bump pointer. That is worth a card rather than a note, and it does not block the desktop
// use this package has today.
//
// **`posix` is narrower than it looks.** The simulation itself touches no operating system: it is
// arithmetic over memory. What reaches POSIX is `timer.c`, which reads a monotonic clock for the
// per-step profile, and `b2World_DumpMemoryStats`, which writes a file. A capability is a property
// of the module rather than of the call (`capabilities.md`), so it is declared here because those
// two are part of the package, and not because an ordinary `step` needs it.

// **What is NOT here is a `requires { headers }` key**, and the absence is the other half of
// vendoring: the constants and every struct layout this binding uses are read out of `box2d/`'s own
// headers by the C compiler, and those headers are carried here beside the C they belong to. Unlike
// `cairo` or the SDL3 packages there is no directory for a consumer to be told about and no
// `--include-path` to forget.
